-- title:
--   Special fibre of the Raynaud quotient as a tensor square
-- statement:
--   The setting is the following. Fixed are a prime $p$ and a modulus $M$ with a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$, together with the hypotheses $p \mid M$ (`hpM`), $p^{2} \nmid M$ (`hpM2`) and `hHp`, which requires every unit of $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ mapping to $1$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^{\times}$ to lie in $H$. Fixed are further a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that $p$ is a non-unit of $Pl$ (`hPl`, the predicate `LiesOverPrime`), whose residue field has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed; the hypothesis `hj` that the $q$-series `jqModC` of $j$ lies in the $q$-expansion function field of the full group $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$; a Deligne–Rapoport type integral model package $\mathfrak{X} :$ `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` over $R_p = \mathbb{Z}$ localised at $p$; level data $\Lambda$ for the curve of level $M/p$, consisting of a structure morphism $\Lambda.f : \Lambda.X \to \operatorname{Spec} R_p$ with a relative group law $\Lambda.L$, a section $\Lambda.\sigma A$ over $Pl$ and identifications of points; and a Néron object $O :$ `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, i.e. a smooth separated group scheme $O.g : O.G \to \operatorname{Spec} R_p$ with relative group law $O.L$, an identification $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of $J_H(M,H) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of $\overline{X_H}$ with sections over the generic point, Hecke correspondences $O.\mathrm{hecke}$, a toric rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, and the subgroups $O.\mathrm{finPts}(m)$ (generated by the $m$-torsion classes whose section extends over the place) and $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(m)$ (generated by the toric points). The hypotheses `hrep` and `hrepΛ` require, respectively, that $(O.G, O.g)$ with its unit section represent the sub-$\mathrm{Pic}^0$ functor of the model of level $\Gamma_M$ cut out by fibrewise algebraically trivial rigidified line bundles (the condition `algEquivZeroCut`), and that $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$ represent the corresponding functor for the model of level $\Gamma_N$, rigidified by $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$.
--
--   The local ring group consists of: a type $R_h$ which is a henselian local domain and a discrete valuation ring, an $R_h$-algebra structure on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with injective structure map, and the hypotheses `hRA` (the image of $R_h$ lies in $Pl$), `hRloc` (an element lies in the maximal ideal of $R_h$ exactly when its image has $Pl$-valuation $< 1$), an $R_h$-algebra structure on $\mathbb{Z}/p$ and `hres` (an element of $R_h$ maps to $0$ in $\mathbb{Z}/p$ exactly when its image has $Pl$-valuation $< 1$). Also fixed are a set $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ and a unit $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ whose image in $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ is $p$ (`hd`).
--
--   The finite-part witness consists of a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ over $R_h$ of height $h$ (finite free Hopf algebras $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transitions whose kernels are the $p^{v}$-torsion ideals), an additive map $\Delta$ from the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathcal{G}$ to $J_H(M,H)$, a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ between the Tate module of those points and the Tate module of $J_H(M,H)$, and the hypotheses: `hΔinj` ($\Delta$ injective); `hΔlev` (for every $v$, an element of $J_H(M,H)$ lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^{v})$ exactly when it is $\Delta$ of a point of $\mathcal{G}$ of level $v$); `hΔgal` ($\Delta$ is equivariant for $\mathrm{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ via any $R_h$-automorphism inducing the same map); `hΔhecke` (for every set $S$ and every Hecke generator $g \in$ [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) there are bialgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, compatible with the transitions, inducing via $\Delta$ the operator `genOpH M H S g` on $J_H(M,H)$); `he` ($e$ is computed componentwise by $\Delta$); `heinj` ($e$ injective); `herange` (a Tate vector lies in the range of $e$ exactly when its $n$-th component lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^{n})$ for all $n$); `hegal` ($e$ intertwines the Tate module representation of $\mathcal{G}$ with `JH.tateGaloisRep`); `hsat` (the range of $e$ is saturated: $p\,y$ in the range implies $y$ in the range); `hcoker` (the cokernel of $e$ is $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linearly isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$); and `htor` (every element of $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^{v})$ is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point).
--
--   The Raynaud quotient group consists of a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $R_h$ of height $h_B$, bialgebra maps $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, a natural number $h'$, and the hypotheses: `hhB` ($h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$); `hhB2` ($h_B = 2h'$); `hψt` (compatibility of the $\psi_v$ with the transitions); `hψker` (a level-$v$ point $x$ of $\mathcal{G}$ becomes trivial in $\mathcal{B}$ exactly when $\Delta x \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^{v})$); `hψsurj` (every level-$v$ $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $\mathcal{B}$ is induced by one of $\mathcal{G}$); `hψred` (if the image of $x$ in $\mathcal{B}$ reduces to the identity, in the sense that $\mathrm{val}_{Pl}(x(a) - \varepsilon(a)) < 1$ for all $a$ in the level algebra, then so does $x$); and `hperiod` (for each $v$, each $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, each $p^{v}$-torsion class $z$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of $\overline{X_H}$ and each level-$v$ point $y$ of $\mathcal{G}$ with $\Delta y = \sigma z - z$, the image of $y$ in $\mathcal{B}$ reduces to the identity in the same valuation-theoretic sense).
--
--   The scheme-theoretic comparison group consists of a ring map $\rho_h : R_p \to R_h$ and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$, subject to: `hρh` ($\rho_h$ followed by $R_h \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is the structure map of $R_p$); `hιbase` ($\iota_v$ followed by $O.g$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R_h \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_h$); `hιcl` (the resulting lift into the pullback of $O.g$ along $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$ is a closed immersion); `hιp` ($\iota_v$ followed by multiplication by $p^{v}$ equals $\iota_v$ followed by $O.g$ and then the unit section); `hιpts` (the section attached by $O.\mathrm{pts}$ to $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point $x$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$); `hιmul` ($\iota_v$ is additive: for points $x,y$ of level $v$ over any $R_h$-algebra $B$ satisfying the evident base conditions, $\operatorname{Spec}(x\cdot y)$ followed by $\iota_v$ is the $O.L$-product of the two corresponding sections); `hιt` (compatibility with the transitions); `hιhecke` (for every $S$ and Hecke generator $g$ there are transition-compatible bialgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ with $\operatorname{Spec}\varphi_v$ followed by $\iota_v$ equal to $\iota_v$ followed by $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,g$, and inducing `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$); and `hιfin` (for each $v$, the morphism $j_v$ obtained from $\iota_v$ by lifting into the pullback presenting the $p^{v}$-torsion of $O.G$ and then into its base change along $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$ is simultaneously an open and a closed immersion, and every point of that pullback lying over the closed point of $R_h$ belongs to the topological range of $j_v$).
--
--   The special-fibre group consists of: an $\mathbb{F}_p$-point $\sigma_p$ of $\operatorname{Spec} R_p$ with `hσp` identifying it with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R_h \to \mathbb{Z}/p$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$; morphisms $\iota^p_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to \operatorname{pullback}(O.g, \sigma_p)$ whose two projections are prescribed by `hιp₁` (the first is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the right inclusion followed by $\iota_v$) and `hιp₂` (the second is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathbb{Z}/p \to \mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$); a pair $q_0, q_1$ of morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$ from the $\sigma_p$-base change of $O.g$ to the $\sigma_p$-base change of $\Lambda.f$; an $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra structure on the residue field of $Pl$ with `hfac` ($\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathbb{Z}/p \to$ residue field followed by $\sigma_p$ equals the residue point of $Pl$ followed by $\Lambda.\sigma A$); `hqmul` (each $q_i$ is a homomorphism for the base-changed group laws of $O.L$ and $\Lambda.L$); and `hqbc` (for each $i$, the morphism `O.abqFibre i` carried by $O$, followed by the canonical comparison of pullbacks along $\mathbb{Z}/p \to$ residue field, equals that comparison followed by $q_i$, so that $q_i$ descends `O.abqFibre i` to $\mathbb{F}_p$). Added to these are `hjB` (for each $v$, the map $\mathrm{id} \otimes \psi_v$ on $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} (-)$ is injective) and a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{A}$ over $R_p$ of height $h_\Lambda$ with: `hBΛ` ($h_B = 2 h_\Lambda$); an $R_p$-algebra structure on $\mathbb{Z}/p$ with `hσp'` ($\sigma_p = \operatorname{Spec}$ of that algebra map); morphisms $\iota'_v : \operatorname{Spec}((\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p).\mathrm{level}\,v) \to \operatorname{pullback}(\Lambda.f, \sigma_p)$ with `hι'base` (the second projection is the structure map), `hι'cl` (each $\iota'_v$ is a closed immersion), `hι'p` (for each $v$ the relation expressing that $\iota'_v$ is killed by $p^{v}$ holds, and the induced lift of $\iota'_v$ into the pullback presenting the $p^{v}$-torsion of the base-changed $\Lambda$ is an isomorphism), `hι'mul` ($\iota'_v$ is additive for points over any $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra) and `hι't` (compatibility with the transitions).
--
--   Under all of these hypotheses the conclusion asserts the existence of morphisms
--   $$r_i(v) : \operatorname{Spec}\bigl((\mathcal{B} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p).\mathrm{level}\,v\bigr) \longrightarrow \operatorname{pullback}(\Lambda.f, \sigma_p), \qquad i \in \{0,1\},\ v \in \mathbb{N},$$
--   and of $\mathbb{F}_p$-bialgebra isomorphisms
--   $$\kappa_v : (\mathcal{B} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p).\mathrm{level}\,v \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; (\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p).\mathrm{level}\,v \otimes_{\mathbb{F}_p} (\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p).\mathrm{level}\,v$$
--   such that the following five assertions hold. First, for each $i \in \{0,1\}$ and each $v$, $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the ring map $\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{F}_p} \otimes \psi_v : \mathbb{F}_p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathbb{F}_p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ followed by $r_i(v)$ equals $\iota^p_v$ followed by $q_i$. Second, for each $v$ this morphism $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{id} \otimes \psi_v)$ is an epimorphism of schemes. Third, for each $v$, $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the composite of the left inclusion $(\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p).\mathrm{level}\,v \to (\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p).\mathrm{level}\,v \otimes_{\mathbb{F}_p} (\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p).\mathrm{level}\,v$ with $\kappa_v^{-1}$, followed by $\iota'_v$, equals $r_0(v)$. Fourth, the same statement with the right inclusion and $r_1(v)$. Fifth, the family $(\kappa_v)$ is compatible with the transition maps: for each $v$, $\kappa_v$ precomposed with the transition map of $\mathcal{B} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p$ at $v$ equals $\kappa_{v+1}$ followed by the tensor product of the transition map of $\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p$ at $v$ with itself, as bialgebra maps from $(\mathcal{B} \otimes \mathbb{F}_p).\mathrm{level}(v+1)$.
--
--   This is the geometric heart of the analysis of the special fibre at $p$ of the Jacobian of $X_H$ when $p$ exactly divides the level: the two component maps to the abelian quotient identify the special fibre of the Raynaud quotient of the finite part with the square of the $p$-power torsion of the $p$-divisible group attached to level $M/p$, level by level and compatibly with transitions. It is used in the construction of the idempotent decomposition of the finite part and in the subsequent extraction of the pair of projectors onto the two components.
